@meta
id: 29-02
title: Loaded now, or loaded when you ask
chapter: 29
type: lesson
skills: packadd
par: 33
minutes: 5
vscode: an extension's activation events, written by somebody else
open: migration.sql
vimrc: set packpath^=. | packloadall!

@brief
This lesson ships two packages, both of them real files in this directory:

    pack/dojo/start/sortimports/plugin/sortimports.vim
    pack/dojo/opt/casing/plugin/casing.vim

The first is in `start` and was loaded before you saw the screen. The
second is in `opt` and has not been loaded at all. `:Snake` does not
exist yet; try it and vim will say so.

`opt` is for plugins you want on the machine but not in every session —
heavy ones, ones with mappings that get in your way, ones you use once a
month. You load them by name, when you want them.

The migration writes camelCase column names. The database uses snake_case
everywhere else. Load the casing plugin and let it fix the whole file. It
sorts out any camelCase word in the range you give it, and its range
defaults to the whole file.

@keys
| `:packadd {name}` | load a package from `pack/*/opt/{name}` right now |
| `:packadd! {name}` | the form for a vimrc: load it, but not while `-u NONE` |
| `:Snake` | what `pack/dojo/opt/casing` defines, once it is loaded |

@hints
The package directory is called `casing`. `:packadd` takes that name —
not a path, not a filename.
--
`:packadd casing`, then the command it defines. Read
`pack/dojo/opt/casing/plugin/casing.vim` if you want to know the range it
takes before you point it at the file.
--
`:packadd casing` `Enter`, then `:%Snake` `Enter`. The `%` is the whole
file, the same `%` as in `:%s`.

@solution
    :packadd casing<Enter>  :%Snake<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

`:packadd` does two things, both boring and both worth knowing. It adds
the package's directory to `runtimepath`, and it sources every `plugin/*.vim`
underneath. After that the package is indistinguishable from one in
`start`, because `start` is just `:packadd` run for you at startup by
`:packloadall`.

Which means the split is entirely yours to decide. A rule that works:

  - `start` — anything whose absence you would notice within a minute.
    Surround, commentary, your colour scheme.
  - `opt` — anything you invoke deliberately. A database client, a
    profiler, a language plugin for the one project that uses it.

In a vimrc you write `packadd! name` with the bang, which registers the
directory but defers sourcing until after your vimrc has finished. Without
the bang, a package loaded from a vimrc gets sourced in the middle of your
config, before the options it reads have been set. That is the origin of a
lot of "it works when I run it by hand but not at startup" bug reports.

Lazy loading, which plugin managers sell hard, is `:packadd` on a trigger.
An autocommand on `FileType sql` that runs `packadd casing` is the whole
idea, in one line, with no manager involved.

@start pack/dojo/start/sortimports/plugin/sortimports.vim
" dojo-sortimports.vim - sort an ES module import block by module path.
" Shipped inside this lesson. You did not install it; the dojo did.

if exists('g:loaded_dojo_sortimports')
  finish
endif
let g:loaded_dojo_sortimports = 1

function! s:sort_imports() abort
  let first = 0
  let last = 0
  for n in range(1, line('$'))
    if getline(n) =~# '^import '
      let first = first == 0 ? n : first
      let last = n
    endif
  endfor
  if first == 0
    echo 'SortImports: no import block found'
    return
  endif
  execute first . ',' . last . 'sort /from /'
endfunction

command! SortImports call s:sort_imports()

@start pack/dojo/opt/casing/plugin/casing.vim
" dojo-casing.vim - rewrite camelCase identifiers as snake_case.
" Shipped inside this lesson, in pack/dojo/opt, so it is NOT loaded until
" you run :packadd casing.

if exists('g:loaded_dojo_casing')
  finish
endif
let g:loaded_dojo_casing = 1

function! s:snake(first, last) abort
  for n in range(a:first, a:last)
    let line = getline(n)
    let out = substitute(line, '\l\+\%(\u\l\+\)\+',
          \ '\=tolower(substitute(submatch(0), "\\(\\l\\)\\(\\u\\)", "\\1_\\2", "g"))', 'g')
    if out !=# line
      call setline(n, out)
    endif
  endfor
endfunction

command! -range=% Snake call s:snake(<line1>, <line2>)

@start migration.sql
CREATE TABLE orders (
  id CHAR(36) NOT NULL,
  customerId CHAR(36) NOT NULL,
  placedAt DATETIME NOT NULL,
  totalMinor INT NOT NULL,
  cancellationReason VARCHAR(64) NULL,
  PRIMARY KEY (id)
);

CREATE INDEX orders_customerId_idx ON orders (customerId);

@expected migration.sql
CREATE TABLE orders (
  id CHAR(36) NOT NULL,
  customer_id CHAR(36) NOT NULL,
  placed_at DATETIME NOT NULL,
  total_minor INT NOT NULL,
  cancellation_reason VARCHAR(64) NULL,
  PRIMARY KEY (id)
);

CREATE INDEX orders_customer_id_idx ON orders (customer_id);
